## Performance Optimizations ✅

The API includes several performance optimizations:

1. **Efficient Database Queries**:
   - Batch requests to minimize database calls
   - Optimized JOINs and relationship handling
   - Selective column fetching

2. **Caching**:
   - Response caching with appropriate cache headers
   - Cache invalidation on content updates
   - Ability to force cache refresh with the `cacheBuster` parameter

3. **Data Loading Optimizations**:
   - N+1 query problem solved with bulk data loading
   - Parallel async data fetching where appropriate
   - Deferred relationship loading when needed

4. **Network Efficiency**:
   - Compressed responses
   - Pagination to limit payload size
   - Only necessary fields returned in responses

## Code Architecture

The implemented solution follows these best practices:

1. **Clean Code Architecture**:
   - Single responsibility principle
   - DRY (Don't Repeat Yourself)
   - Modular design with helper functions

2. **Type Safety**:
   - TypeScript throughout
   - Proper interface definitions
   - Runtime type validation

3. **Error Handling**:
   - Comprehensive try/catch blocks
   - Detailed error messages
   - Graceful fallbacks

4. **Security**:
   - Input validation
   - Authentication for protected endpoints
   - Rate limiting
   - No direct database access from the headless site

## Example Usage

### JavaScript Fetch API
```javascript
// Fetch latest posts
async function fetchLatestPosts() {
  const response = await fetch('https://api.example.com/api/public/blog?resource=posts&limit=5');
  const data = await response.json();
  return data.posts;
}

// Fetch a single post
async function fetchPost(slug) {
  const response = await fetch(`https://api.example.com/api/public/blog?resource=post&slug=${slug}`);
  return await response.json();
}

// Fetch categories with post counts
async function fetchCategories() {
  const response = await fetch('https://api.example.com/api/public/blog?resource=categories');
  return await response.json();
}
```

### React Component Example
```jsx
import { useState, useEffect } from 'react';

function BlogList() {
  const [posts, setPosts] = useState([]);
  const [loading, setLoading] = useState(true);
  const [error, setError] = useState(null);
  
  useEffect(() => {
    async function fetchData() {
      try {
        setLoading(true);
        const response = await fetch('/api/public/blog?resource=posts&limit=10');
        const data = await response.json();
        
        if (data.error) {
          throw new Error(data.error);
        }
        
        setPosts(data.posts || []);
      } catch (err) {
        setError(err.message);
      } finally {
        setLoading(false);
      }
    }
    
    fetchData();
  }, []);
  
  if (loading) return <div>Loading...</div>;
  if (error) return <div>Error: {error}</div>;
  if (posts.length === 0) return <div>No posts found</div>;
  
  return (
    <div>
      <h1>Latest Blog Posts</h1>
      <div className="posts-grid">
        {posts.map(post => (
          <div key={post.id} className="post-card">
            {post.featuredImage && (
              <img src={post.featuredImage} alt={post.title} />
            )}
            <h2>{post.title}</h2>
            <p>{post.excerpt}</p>
            <a href={`/blog/${post.slug}`}>Read more</a>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  );
}
```

### Real-time Updates with WebSockets
```javascript
// Subscribe to real-time updates
function subscribeToUpdates(callback) {
  const socket = new WebSocket('wss://api.example.com/ws');
  
  socket.onopen = () => {
    console.log('Connected to WebSocket server');
    socket.send(JSON.stringify({
      type: 'subscribe',
      resource: 'blog_posts'
    }));
  };
  
  socket.onmessage = (event) => {
    const data = JSON.parse(event.data);
    callback(data);
  };
  
  socket.onerror = (error) => {
    console.error('WebSocket error:', error);
  };
  
  socket.onclose = () => {
    console.log('WebSocket connection closed');
    // Attempt to reconnect after a delay
    setTimeout(() => subscribeToUpdates(callback), 5000);
  };
  
  return {
    unsubscribe: () => socket.close()
  };
}

// Usage
const subscription = subscribeToUpdates((data) => {
  console.log('Real-time update received:', data);
  // Update UI or trigger a refresh
});

// Cleanup when component unmounts
subscription.unsubscribe();
```

## Webhook Integration

Headless sites can receive real-time updates when content changes by subscribing to webhooks. Here's how to implement a webhook receiver:

```javascript
// Express.js webhook receiver
const express = require('express');
const app = express();
app.use(express.json());

app.post('/webhook/blog-updates', (req, res) => {
  const { event, resource, resourceId, timestamp } = req.body;
  
  console.log(`Event received: ${event} for ${resource} ${resourceId}`);
  
  // Validate webhook signature
  const signature = req.headers['x-webhook-signature'];
  if (!verifySignature(signature, req.body)) {
    return res.status(401).json({ error: 'Invalid signature' });
  }
  
  // Handle different event types
  switch (event) {
    case 'post.published':
    case 'post.updated':
      // Clear cache or update in-memory store
      clearCacheForPost(resourceId);
      break;
    case 'post.deleted':
      // Remove from cache or store
      removeDeletedPost(resourceId);
      break;
    // Handle other event types...
  }
  
  res.status(200).json({ received: true });
});

app.listen(3000, () => {
  console.log('Webhook receiver listening on port 3000');
});
```

## Caching Strategies

The API implements several caching strategies:

1. **HTTP Caching**:
   - `Cache-Control` headers with appropriate max-age
   - ETag support for conditional requests
   - Cache-busting with the `cacheBuster` parameter

2. **Content Delivery Network (CDN)**:
   - Configure your CDN to respect the cache headers
   - Purge CDN cache on webhook events

3. **Client-side Caching**:
   - Implement local storage caching in the headless site
   - Invalidate cache on webhook notifications

## Error Handling

All endpoints return standard HTTP status codes:
- 200: Success
- 400: Bad Request (invalid parameters)
- 401: Unauthorized
- 404: Not Found
- 429: Too Many Requests (rate limiting)
- 500: Server Error

## Security Considerations ✅

1. **Authentication and Authorization**:
   - Public endpoints provide read-only access to published content
   - Management endpoints require authentication
   - Role-based access control for internal dashboards

2. **Input Validation**:
   - All parameters are validated before use
   - Proper escaping of user-provided search terms

3. **Rate Limiting**:
   - API endpoints are rate-limited to prevent abuse
   - Graduated rate limits based on client needs

4. **Security Headers**:
   - CORS headers to control access
   - Content-Security-Policy
   - X-Content-Type-Options

